Paul Lew (lew@omocha.tay.dec.com) wrote:
: When I dial into our DEC terminal server, I have the choice of
: specifying type of protocol (telnet, lat, ppp, slip).  If I choose the
: default 'connect (LAT)' then it does not recognize the Unix machine I
: specified.  Do I have to setup LAT server on the Unix machine in order
: to use the 'connect' protocol?  The other problem is not all the
: machines I tried to connect are DEC machines and how can I use the
: 'connect (LAT)' protocol for those machines?

: If I choose telnet, I can telnet into any of the Unix machines but the
: control characters are mapped (^X, ^Y, ^T are eaten up by the terminal
: telnet server).  I used 'set port telnet client ...' commands to
: disable all the default mapping but no matter what I tried I can not
: let it pass LF and ^O char to my Unix machine.  Any help will be
: appreciated...

On Ultrix, LAT has to be compiled into the kernel, the LAT ttys must
be defined and turned on in /etc/ttys, and the LAT Control Program,
/etc/lcp, must be started in /etc/rc.local.  If any of these steps
are omitted, it won't work.

To check the kernel, look in /sys/conf/vax/HOSTNAME for evidence
of LAT in the kernel options.  To check for LAT ttys, look for
16 instances of /dev/ttynn on major 39.  Look on /etc/ttys to see
if they are listed and turned on in the getty listener.  Run lcp
in the diagnostic mode to see if LAT is running.

Barry Kort


