Now, now, R. D.  it pains me as much as the next old mini-collector to
see these old beasts go to the smelters flame, but it is not the
smelters fault.  He holds the same spot in the food chain as the
tallow guy who buys horse carcasses.  Doesn't mean he can't love
horses.  I have a great relationship with a couple of different
scrap-metal dealers.  Computer breakage is usually $0.50/lb whole,
$1.00/lb for just cards &cetera.  There are few things as wonderful as
bringing a Sun 4/200 CPU board to the guy at the counter, saying "How
much you want for this?" And having him throw it on a meat scale and
say, "Three bucks."  (Unfortunately, this guy knows what memory chips
sell for, so I wouldn't be able to do that with a 32Meg memory card.)

There's this one junkyard I know about in rural California, who for
many many years had the Pacific Bell scrap contract.  He dealt
EXCLUSIVELY in old Step and XB Central Office equipment.  Tons of
brass and copper in those old beasts.  Kinda eerie walking around
amongst a pile of XB selectors.  He also does computer stuff -- I got
about 5 Sun 3/100 CPUs from him for $10. (total)  The saddest thing I
have ever seen is in one corner of his lot, a pile of "china red" rack
steel.  I asked him what it had been -- it had been a 2040 from a
nearby Military installation.  ~weep~.  Had about 12 RP06s last time I
visited him -- but they've been sitting out in the rain & other such
weather.  

I suppose I should write an article on how to approach these people.
They're usually a really cantakerous group to bargain with -- and
they're always concerned that you're up to something fishy -- "Why on
EARTH does anyone want OLD computers," he'll ask a dozen times.  I
know one place that gets REALLY nervous if you want to buy a machine
whole -- some scrap dealers are under contractual obligation to
"render unusable" any equipment they take in scrap, but a 12-slot VME
cabinet without boards or power supply is a $20 piece of "scrap iron"
and a 850W power supply is a $5 "Computer Power Supply".  

