Dead right - I am with you 100%!  This is one of the places where
VMS (and, still more, IBM MVS) beats the living daylights out of
UNIX :-)

Ever tried handling tape files on UNIX?  In addition to having to
use ghastly and non-portable programs to position and set options,
you find that many standard commands just don't work at all.  On
one UNIX system (which shall remain nameless), cpio refuses to
write to a tape unless it is piped through a tape-dependent filter,
whereas tar is the exact opposite.  Neither is documented :-(

And as for copying a plain text file to a printer, forget it!
Note that lpr ISN'T the printer - it is the filter.  You cannot
simply use the C 'FILE *file = fopen("lpr","w");' the way that
you can on MVS.

I agree with you that I find basic UNIX I/O programming much
easier than the MVS Access Methods because of the more consistent
structuring but, oh, how I long for the device independence that
I used to have!


