In article <3it22h$9h1@news.service.uci.edu>, fliu@uci.edu (Feng Liu) writes:

>We are buying a Seagate ST15230N internal drive to replace a small
>user disk on our DEC 3000/300LX. I was told it was better to 
>partition this 4.1GB drive into smaller partitions and some systems can
>not even handle partitioned more than 2.GB. Is this the case with the  
>DEC3000/300LX?

No.  DEC OSF/1 is currently limited 128 GB device on V3.0 and V3.2 (32 GB
before V3.0).  And the significant reason for that limit is because that's
what's been tested.  The UFS implementation can probably deal with something
up in TB neighborhood.

Some UNIX systems are limited to 2 GB partitions and others are limited
to 4 GB, but we're not one of them.  The typical reason for the limit
is that the byte offset within a file is limited the size of a long
or int which is typically 32 bits.  Since -1 is used an error indication
some vendors only support 31 bits.

Since many file system utilties treat devices as file when reading
and writing the file system data structures, they're limited 2 or 4
GB devices.  Systems that support 64 bit offsets don't have this
problem.

>If anybody has a working disktab file for this drive, I'd appreciate if
>you can e-mail a copy to me.

For most uses, the disklabel command can get the information it needs
from the disk driver.  Newfs will then use the information from the
label.

	# disklabel -wr /dev/rrz#c ST15230N
	# newfs /dev/rrz#c

