The largest difference between the two boards is amount of VRAM.

* ZLX(p)-E2  =  8 MB VRAM
* ZLX(p)-E3  = 16 MB VRAM

From that VRAM comes all your resources such as on-screen VRAM, and
off-screen resources such as Z-Buffer, backbuffer, pixmaps, etc etc etc....

So, let's configure the ZLX(p)-E2 at 1280x1024 pixels.  That consumes
32 bits/pixel or 4 bytes/pixel for on-screen memory, or about 5 MB
out of that 8 MB VRAM.  That leaves you with 3 MB of VRAM for most
other resources.  (BTW, if you are doing 1024x768 pixels, you'll have
less on screen and more off-screen, but the principle is the same.)

If you desire a 24/24 Zbuffered large window, you can see that you are
going to run out and something will give.  So that gives you an
upper limit for size of window that gets that VRAM.  Otherwise,
some resources will be on the host.  (A SW Zbuffer btw is not
the end of the world in general.  A SW backbuffer, however, is not
something to aim for, *ever*.)


Since you are working with students, they probably will learn OpenGL
just fine thanks with 12/12 double buffered, z buffered visuals with
dithering enabled.  (12/12 dithered is very very good indeed.)

The front and back buffers for 12/12 can come out of on-screen resources
in this case, so you have room for a 3 MB VRAM Z buffer and other things.
Not bad at all.


*BUT*, you will probably have to explain things to a student from
time to time.  Such as why two students sitting next to each other
are getting different performance for their applications.  Maybe
one is using the Utah Raster Toolkit to put up an image of
whatever.  If they don't use "getx11 -s" (for stingy, use client
image, not server pixmap) they have probably eaten up some of
that offscreen VRAM for that very valuable image of - whatever.


That is, count everything that might use VRAM resources, not just
your student's OpenGL application.  Pay particular attention to
pixmaps and other server extensions that might like VRAM resources.
Close enough 

If your students don't often use double buffer, then that gives
you much more breathing space as well.  (In an OpenGL course?
They'll more than likely be double buffering.)



So, it's your call.  If you know that students are going to be
going with moderate size windows, and/or moderate depth windows,
then you can put that $1,000 toward faster CPU's or more memory or....

If you aren't sure that you have enough VRAM for most cases, then you'll
have to weigh the value of explaining to students why performance may
vary somewhat - or may even vary dramatically.  Then again, this could
be a feature!

But if you anticipate that students will be doing near full screen
24 bit true color double buffer depth buffer work, or you are sure
that 8 MB VRAM is not enough, then you really most likely do want
the ZLX(p)-E3.

