It's been several years since I did anything major with LAT servers, but
I'll take a stab at answering your question anyway.

One reason why uploads are slow is the general design of the LAT protocol.
LAT, in general is designed to carry a lot of data outbound (from host
computers to terminals), with a relatively small amount of inbound traffic.
The design model is the user sitting in front of a terminal. The host
computer typically sends many times more characters down the line than it
receives up the line from the terminal user typing away.

Consequently, if you put a datascope on an Ethernet and watch the packets
during a download, you'll see fewer LAT datagrams with more packets, but on
an upload there'll be many smaller packets with fewer characters.  Also,
the LAT protocol implements a "credits" scheme, whereby each end of the LAT
session (host and server) grant each other a certain number of credits. The
number of credits granted represents how many packets can be received
without overrunning that end's receive buffers. Each time a packet is sent,
the credits available are decremented. When the other end processes the
packet (moving it out of the receive buffers and up to the next software
layer [usually the terminal driver], a credit is sent back.  If the
terminal server runs out of credits, it will stop sending packets to the
host until it receives more credits.

So, the credit mechanism can be responsible for the poor performance, since
each packet sent up to the host costs a credit, no matter how small.


