In article <3igdou$1k3@ccshst05.cs.uoguelph.ca>, 
rmacklem@uoguelph.ca (Rick Macklem) writes:

>We are looking at low end AXP workstations for use in teaching OpenGL and
>was wondering what the difference between the ZLX-E2 and ZLX-E3 display
>boards really is? The difference seems to be that the ZLX-E3 has a Z-buffer,
>whereas the ZLX-E2 does not. 

More accurately, the -E3 has more on-board memory which can be used for 
Z-buffer or other things.

>However, since there doesn't seem to be any
>3D hardware accelerator on either card, wouldn't the Z-buffer just be kept
>in the X server's virtual memory for the ZLX-E2? (I'm no graphics wiz, so
>I am just guessing on this one:-)

Neither board has a geometry accelerator; both have very fast rasterizing
engines. The host CPU does all the 3D work and passes off lines to the
rasterizing chip. This means that when you increase the performance of
the host CPU you get faster graphics -- we've been bitten before by geometry
accelerators that are slower than new host CPUs.

The rasterizing chip also does z-buffer work if the z-buffer is on the
graphics board. However, if the z-buffer is in host memory than the host CPU
must do much more work -- resulting in slower speed.  For small windows on
the -E2, everything will fit on board -- but make the window larger and you
force the z-buffer into host memory and the rendering into host software
and everything s l o w s  d o w n. 

>The bottom line is, "Is the ZLX-E3 worth the extra $1,000"?

If you are doing large window, double buffered, z-buffered, graphics then
the -E3 is definitely work the extra cost. If you are willing to put up
with less performance on this case an -E2 will give good performance
up to the limits of the on-board memory.

Andy V (A Digital spoke, not a Digital spokesperson)


