> digital equipment. The linker is Windows NT Linker Ver. 2.43

Between 3.1 and 3.5 we corrected some errors in the format
section attributes used by the Alpha compiler.  The linker
gets confused when it sees both old and new objects, and
can make bad executable files.  The error you've quoted is one
such symptom.

I think it's possible that you are using more recent libraries
and that those are confusing the linker (because the version
of the compiler you have is producing old objects).

The short answer to your problem is to get Visual C++ V2.0 for
Alpha.  It will solve your problem.

The compiler you have was shipped in September 1993.
It's quite old (obsolete).  Subsequent compiler are much much
better.  Many bugs in the initial compiler got fixed.
The code generated is also much smaller and faster.
Debugging has also been improved.  And with the Visual
product you get a whole bunch of nice tools.

Since Microsoft (with Digital's help) shipped Visual C++ V2.0
for Alpha, compilers are nolonger included with SDKs.  [Sorry,
this is Microsoft's descision, not ours.  The change matches
x86 and MIPS.  The nice Visual tools do make up somewhat for
the cost of the compiler, though.]

Roland.

