I'm trying to run a Beta version of Windows 95 on a new Digital
HiNote CS450 but I'm having serious problems with the serial
ports. Now I know that this is an unreleased operating system,
and that it is reasonable to expect problems, but surely I am
not the only one in the world who has come across this.

The symptoms are that the whole machine locks up whenever I
try to use the serial port. It does not matter what application
I use -- WinQVT, Windows HyperTerminal, Dial-up Networking.
With the October Beta 2, it would lock as soon as I typed a
character. With the latest beta (build 337) it locks after
a period of time, ranging from a second to maybe 30 seconds,
and possibly depending on how much I type (just talking to the
modem). It always seems to lock immeditately when the other end
answers if I dial out.

I have run the PNPBIOS.EXE program which is one of the Plug and
Play diagnostics available on the Microsoft FTP server, and it
tells me that there are two Serial Ports in the machine, even
though there is only one. Also, the Plug and Play Device IDs for
the two ports are the same (PNP0500).

So here is the question (to Digital if they are listening):
Should there be two serial ports in the Plug 'n' Play BIOS,
and if so, are they allowed to have the same ID or is this
a violation of the Plug 'n' Play spec?

BTW, for those who have been having problems with the Power
Management, viz the machine shuts down unexpectedly when the
battery indicator says you have half a battery left, this is
a "known" problem and will be fixed by an update to the Flash
BIOS.

