>Since AmigaOS doesn't do memory management (i.e. no memory protection)
>context switching is a little easier, and the OS isn't in the same category
>as the others listed (I am assuming QNX and HP-RT do memory protection, and
>know that HP-UX of course does)  Still not a bad time though, considering it
>runs on a 5 year old CPU...

A problem with this benchmark is that it attempts to measure context switch 
intervals using signals rather than the POSIX sched_yield() call, in order 
to be portable to OS's lacking sched_yield().  As a result, it ends up 
measuring the combination of signal processing AND context switching, not 
just context switching.  Since AmigaOS does not support UNIX-style signals, 
the benchmark was recoded to be a pure context switch measurement on the 
Amiga, so the number that results does not represent the same metric.  By 
way of example, a QNX benchmark coded using sched_yield(), rather than 
signals, results in a 2 microsecond time on a P90, rather than 23 
microseconds.  Since that 2 usec number doesn't represent the same interval 
as for the other OS's, it wouldn't be appropriate to put it into this list.

For these reasons, the author of the benchmark (Larry McVoy, lm@sgi.com) 
posted a remark some time ago indicating that he would prefer that this 
benchmark not be quoted as a measurement of context switch time (since it 
isn't).  He's produced a MUCH better measurement tool in the form of 
lmbench.  It will be interesting to see how the lmbench numbers for various 
OS's look once they start coming out.
