In article <D4KK92.11A@festival.ed.ac.uk>, jlothian@castle.ed.ac.uk (J Lothian) writes:
> I've tried dropping bits into the control store from the console and
> reading them back. Every third and fourth longword comes back 
> severely mangled, with the topmost of the 20 bits jammed at 1 and 
> several of the lower bits jammed at 0. The bits jammed at 0 seem to 
> vary randomly.
> 
> I've tried reverse-engineering the PCS board, but with very little 
> success.

  First, a bit of history on the PCS - the 750 ("Comet") was originally
designed with ROM-based microcode, unlike the earlier 780 system which
used a combination of ROM-based and floppy-based microcode. However, as
the 750 was quite early in the evolution of the VAX line, the architecture
was undergoing revisions (as well as bugs being fixed in the 750's imple-
mentation). Finally DEC got tired of upgrading the microcode ROMs on the
system and added the PCS - Patchable Control Store. This was enough RAM
that could be mapped on top of the microcode ROMs to implement most needed
patches. Only when the PCS ran out of space would new ROMs be issued. This
was all an afterthought. There is a plug-in card that sticks on the back-
plane pins on the rear of the card cage that sets the CPU revision, etc.
I believe the latest revision/PCS was 104 or thereabouts. As long as you
aren't trying to use newer features or boundary conditions, any 90-something
or higher control store rev (before patching) should be fine.

> As far as I can see, I'm going to have to do one of the following 
> to get past this problem:
> 1) fix the PCS;
> 2) persuade BSD not to load it. 
> 
> Anybody got any ideas?

  The system *might* run without the PCS card. It's been a long time since
my 750 (spcvxc.spc.edu) retired (some guys from Pitt took it away to play
with), so I'm not sure.

  Do you have another system which can read your boot media (presumably a
9-track tape)? If so, you might be able to "dd" the mini-root to a disk
file, use something like "beav" to search for "pcs750" and edit it to some
other name. Then dd the file back to (a scratch) tape, making sure to pre-
serve the original block size.

  You should be able to get a replacement PCS module for next to nothing -
if I had to buy one today (instead of waiting for someone to offer me another
whole 750) I'd only have to pay US $100 for it. (US $100 seems to be the
asking price whenever you need an obsolete module in a hurry). I know of a
reseller here in the US who does international sales, though I expect that's
a bit extreme for a hobby system. However, if you want the info, just write.

