In article <3i0mrh$fjh@hermes.ridgefield.sdr.slb.com> lovell@slb.com (John Lovell) writes:

> Hi, this is probably a dumb question, but how do I change the
> .> key so that it gives a > in "upper case".

One way is to use the keymap editor.  The other way is to
create a file ~/.xmodmaprc

keysym comma = comma less
keysym period = period greater
keycode 0xBF = 0x1B  
keycode 0xC9 = grave asciitilde
keycode 0xB1 = Meta_L
keycode 0xAD = Meta_R
keycode 0xB1 = 0x1B
keycode 0xAD = 0x1B

This is what I use, it does the comma and period fix,
	makes the labeled `~ the ESC key,
	      the labeled <> the `~ key.
	And defines compose-character as a meta (for emacs.)

Delete any of these features you don't want, then in a shell, type

xmodmap ~/.xmodmaprc

If you put that line in ~/.X11Startup it will be done
for you when you log in.

