I think that flow control between the modem and a computer is
determined by request from the modem. Here is my justification.
I also have a DEC Alpha 3000/300 running OSF3.0 and a MultiTech
2834BA modem.  When the first time I connected my modem with
my Alpha with two D25-D9 cables,  the login prompt and anything
output were garbled.   This is a sure sign that the flow control
from the modem is not right.  I then changed the modem setting
(from the default RTS/CTS) to XON/XOFF,   the garbled character
problem was gone and I could log in!   I then changed to a D25-D25
straight through cable  and set back the flow control on my modem
to RTS/CTS.   Now, everything is fine.

So,  see, OSF3.0 has nothing to do with above flow control problem.
It is my modem's problem.

Are you sure that your modem is set right?

