Do you mean VAXstation 2000?  The VS200 is about the size of
a shoebox (OK, a large shoebox).  Even without drives, they
can be booted over the network, and if you have the VR260
graphics monitors for them, you can run DECwindows on them.
I don't know if you can boot them from a non-DEC host, but
if you can find a MicroVAX or VS3100 to act as a server, 
you'd be back in business.

What I usually do with the VS2000 is bring them up with a VT100
console on the printer port and run barebones Ultrix with PPP
added in.  Since the VS2000 has a real serial port, with hardware
flow control, they make nice PPP gateways.  With an RD53, I pare
down the 'b' partition from 50K blocks to 32K blocks, and enlarge
the 'g' partition to hold all of /usr and /usr/var comfortably.

The problem with running them diskless is that you need about
50MB on the server for each diskless VS2000, plus a common /usr
area for all of them to share.

Barry Kort


