for my research, i am seeking actual numerical data for context-switch
times for a variety of architectures and OSes.  i am also seeking data
on cache-miss percentages for transaction processing benchmarks
(e.g. TPC). 

specifically, i am interested in the user-to-kernel context-switch
times, including all overhead incurred in hardware and software
once a syscall, trap, or interrupt occurs until the kernel actually
begins servicing the syscall/trap/interrupt.  with respect to TPC
cache-miss ratios, i'm particularly interested in cold-cache effects
(compulsory misses) after a context switch.
 
i'm also interested in user-to-user context-switch times.

if anyone can provide either actual examples (preferably with a
reference; if collected personally please identify yourself and your
organization and tell me if i can cite you personally) or references
for same (papers, conference proceedings, specs, anything), please
*email me directly*.

it would be of further use if the context-switch time were broken into
actual hardware save/restore time, OS housekeeping/scheduling time,
and/or cold-cache effects (or any combination) but i can work with a
single lumped context-switch time.

please state the conditions under which the context switch was timed
and/or transaction processing cache miss ratio observed;
architecture and OS at a minimum.  if the time includes
syscall/service routine execution time, please state that and whether
it's an avg/best case/worst case.  i'm looking more for a general
picture than for great precision; hence even if your example is
anecdotal i'd like to hear it.

this information is being used to motivate research for new
