In article <3i07lf$sv6@cerberus-138.wustl.edu>, ponder@dasher.wustl.edu (Jay Ponder) writes...
> 
>I'm trying to benchmark a Fortran program on the network available
>Alpha-based OpenVMS system (axpvms.pa.dec.com), but I need to know
>the OpenVMS way of getting the elapsed CPU time for a process from
>Fortran. The OpenVMS Fortran does not support "etime" as does the
>OSF/1 version. I used to have a small hunk of VAX/VMS Fortran code
>that used a call to SYS$GETJPI to get the elapsed time. It worked
>seven years ago, but seems to fail now. Since I don't have access
>to the required OpenVMS docs, could some kind sole help me out....
> 

    I usually use LIB$INIT_TIMER and either LIB$STAT_TIMER or
    LIB$SHOW_TIMER depending on whether I want the values for use
    inside the program or just displayed on SYS$OUTPUT.

    LIB$INIT_TIMER [context]

    LIB$STAT_TIMER code, value-argument [,handle-address]

        code - longword, read only by reference
               1 is elapsed time (quadword system time format)
	       2 is CPU time (longword in 10ms increments)
	       3 is Buffered I/O (longword)
	       4 is Direct I/O (longword)
	       5 is Page faults (longword)
	value-argument - unspecified, write only by reference
               address of the location to receive the result
	handle-address - longword, read only by reference
               same as context if used in LIB$INIT_TIMER (used to get
	       stats for different threads of execution)

    LIB$SHOW_TIMER
	[handle-address][,code][,user-action-procedure][,user-argument-value]

        handle-address - longword, read only be reference
	code - longword, read only by reference
	user-action-procedure - procedure entry mask by value
            if specified, routined is entered with two arguments.  The
	    first is the character string by descriptor containing the
	    buffer that would have been written to SYS$OUTPUT.  The
	    second is the user-argument-value, by value.
	user-argument-value - unspecified, read only be value

        omitting all arguments causes all 5 code values to be
	displayed to SYS$OUTPUT

        e.g.
!
!       Set up to measure
!
	STATUS = LIB$INIT_TIMER()
	STATUS = LIB$SHOW_TIMER()    ! Show initial values in case we
	                             ! has a page fault or a clock
				     ! tick was charged to us
               .
	       .
	       .            ! work to me measured
	       .
        STATUS = LIB$SHOW_TIMER()    ! Show ending values

