Fortunately, things are moving fast though and a self-hosting
Linux/Alpha doesn't seem to be very far away anymore.

 >: 3) to boot to it you'll need OSF/1 firmware.

For the AXPpci33 board (aka Noname), you can get the firmware via
ftp:
	ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com:/pub/DEC/axppci

 >I thought I saw once in some posts that Linus has 150 MHz Alpha 
 >sitting on his desk...

There are two, somewhat distinct porting efforts: the people at DEC
are creating a 32-bit Linux/Alpha version.  That is, both kernel and
user-level will execute in a 32-bit address space only.  A 64-bit
integer data type is available as "long long" in GNU C.  This allows
very rapid porting, as Linux applications are 32-bit already.

In constrast, Linus is working on a 64-bit version.  To ease
transition, it is likely that this kernel will provide the ability to
run 32-bit applications, but ultimately, it will be a full 64-bit
system.  Fortunately, on the Alpha, it is pretty easy to support
32-bit applications with a 64-bit kernel (i.e., no nasty mode
switching or anything---you just compile the application with the C
compiler's -m32 switch, tell the linker to use the 32-bit libraries
and link everything into the low 32-bit of the address space and
that's it).  Linus is also splitting platform dependencies from the
original code.  All combined, this means that it is going to take a
while before this kernel is ready for prime-time.

