In article <DUGAN.95Feb18134321@bucrf16.bu.edu>, dugan@bucrf16.bu.edu (Mike Dugan) writes:
>Last summer I installed Ultrix 4.4 on several DECstation 3100's.
>Since then one of the machines has been logging messages like:
>
> ----- EVENT INFORMATION -----
> [...]
> PROCESSOR TYPE                          KN01
> MESSAGE                                 Cache parity error count for cpu 0 is
>                                         _47864
>
>What does this mean? And what, if anything, can I do about it?

A "cache parity error" is just that.  Cache has a parity bit.  When reading
something out of cache, the parity was wrong, so the system forced a cache miss
(causing the read to be fulfilled from main memory).  It'd be nice if the
system recorded some more information than just a count of errors, but I
suppose it's better than nothing.

When this problem occurs on a DECstation 5000, I replace the cpu module and the
problem is fixed.  I don't remember the internal organization of the 3100, so I
can't offer a FRU.  However, it looks like you're going to have to replace some
FRU on your system.

