Not very surprising!

It *should* be pretty well known anywhere that standardized benchmarks have
merits insofar that they *should* allow to compare OS's, Hardware etc.,
**BUT** that a certain amount of "optimization" for such benchmarks is
often considered worth time and money..... 

I have found that the results of SPECint and SPECfp ratings is very often 
**NOT** repeated if you do real tasks on complicated, CPU intensive tasks.
Some tests hgave been run here using "production" programs (Monte Carlo 
simulations of complex nuclear physics experiments) and found some really 
significant deviations from the prediction of the above mentioned benchmarks.

Be aware, however, that just something as "simple" as different compiler
versions can in some cases perform much different. And even simpler:
Just the quotas of your account can make a real difference in performance, 
for example give you all the memory you need in one piece or make the 
machine paging ...

