In article <D4007w.695@world.std.com>,
shannon@world.std.com (Terry C Shannon) writes:
shannon> OpenVMS harder to use than Unix?  Methinks your friends are
shannon> misinformed!  In fact, system administration is one area in
shannon> which OpenVMS remains (in my opinion) far superior to DEC
shannon> OSF/1.

[ Sigh--here goes another of those Unix vs. VMS wars... ]

Which is easier to administer, Unix or VMS, is a religious issue.  It
depends on what you are used to, which you grew up with, and on your
overall outlook on system administration.

VMS is generally superior if you like having everything documented in
detail in the manuals, and you appreciate having a small number of
well defined interfaces that have to be remembered to accomplish most
administration tasks.

Administering a Unix system tends to involve a lot more mucking about
in the low-levels, looking at the source to figure out what the
documentation meant to say, lots of different commands and switches
for common administration tasks, and lots of rolling your own tools.
However, once you've gotten past the miserable bits, there's a lot
of power and flexibility available from understanding how all those
fiddly bits go together.

Each clearly shows its heritage--VMS was always targeted more at
production environments, while Unix spent most of its formative years
in research.  Unix has been growing into more of a production system
in recent years, but most versions still have a ways to go.

I've worked with and sometimes administered VMS and Unix systems--VMS
since 1979 (version 1.6), Unix since 1988.  I personally find Unix
systems a lot more fun--but I might feel differently if I worked in a
more commercial environment.

