Steve Groom (stevo@elroy.jpl.nasa.gov) wrote:
: I'm looking for some advice about using a DEC DR11-W (well, an emulator
: version running in a Sun desktop workstation) to talk to a device which
: only understands the older DR11-C parallel interface.  If you don't
: know what I'm talking about, you can forget the rest of this message,
: but thanks anyway. :-)

It should be doable.  The DR11-W has 16 bits out, 16 bits in and three
control lines out and three status lines in.  I think the DR11-C is similar
but without the DMA.  I am more familiar with the Q-bus versions than unibus.
I built a device to do memory-memory transfers using a DRV11-WA on one
end, and another device to control an old graphics workstation, also using
a drv11-wa (similar to DR11-W but for q-bus).  I don't know the details of
what makes a DR11-C different from a DR-11.  Do you have some info on the
specs for the DR11-C?  I could then tell you what might be different about
it from the data and control/status on the DR11-W.

Anyway, you get 3 control lines out, 3 status lines in, and data in and out
on 2 separate sets of 16 signal wires with the DR11-W and anything that 
emulates it.  On a real DEC DR11-W you can wiggle any output line 
independently (with a little anding and oring on the control registers).
You have complete control of the lines without using the DMA part of
the interface.  I used this to set up the remote devices before initiating
the DMA transfer.  So, you should be able to use the SUN interface to do
what you need.


