result.  I managed this in 5 minutes on a VAX running VMS.  (Incidentally,
the worst machine by this metric was a Burroughs, which took me 3 days to
get it to add 3 numbers!)  The first time I sat down in front of a Sun with
a username and password, I couldn't even figure out how to log off the stupid
thing.

No, Antony.  Unix is simply user-hostile.  If you and all your users have
already struggled with it until you've become experts, then certainly you
mightn't care to spend even a little time learning VMS.  Too bad, because
it's still among the best I've seen.  (I liked TOPS-20 too.)  However, as
a Unix partisan, be aware of the following myths about VMS which I believe
to be patently untrue:

1)  Unix is easier to learn than VMS
2)  Unix is easier to administer
3)  Commands are shorter in Unix
4)  Programs are easier to port to Unix platforms
5)  A simple stream-lf file system is better
6)  Unix machines are faster than VMS machines
7)  Unix machines are cheaper than VMS machines
8)  Unix machines are open and VMS is closed
9)  VMS is dead

OK, I've now run out of steam here.  VMS partisans note: I regard all of
the above 1-9 statements as false to outrageously false, yet I've heard them
all from the brainwashed Unix crowd.

Jonathan Boswell
