> I'm trying to benchmark a Fortran program on the network available
> Alpha-based OpenVMS system (axpvms.pa.dec.com), but I need to know
> the OpenVMS way of getting the elapsed CPU time for a process from
> Fortran. The OpenVMS Fortran does not support "etime" as does the
> OSF/1 version. I used to have a small hunk of VAX/VMS Fortran code
> that used a call to SYS$GETJPI to get the elapsed time. It worked
> seven years ago, but seems to fail now. Since I don't have access
> to the required OpenVMS docs, could some kind sole help me out....

        It's  pretty  easy  to  do  with  the  Run-time  Library routine
    LIB$STAT_TIMER.  Look at the online HELP under:

        	      $ HELP RTL LIB$ LIB$STAT_TIMER

    Read the description a  bit  carefully:  you'll  notice that the CPU
    time  is returned in a 4-byte integer in units of 10  millisceconds.
    Also look  at  LIB$INIT_TIMER,  which  must  be  called  first,  and
    LIB$FREE_TIMER if you're doing more complicated things...

        -Ken
