Huh?  Why would you want to kick out ultrix?  I used a DECstation 2100
as an X-terminal all summer working on a SGI box. It only ran the WM
and X locally (plus a few other bits and pieces..) and I used it
almost solely as an X terminal.  Worked fine for me.

My suggestion is that you install X on it (if you haven't done that
already), strip the system down to the bare bones and then you have an
X-terminal.

If you can afford having a DECstation 5000 as a dedicated X terminal
(I wouldn't believe it to be very fast at that though), that's great,
but I would try to find some better use for it.  (Around here we let
"old" machines run low-traffic information systems or we donate the
systems to student societies).
Actually I would think a PC with a nice video-board and a nice screen
would give you "more bang for the buck". (Running Linux for example).

Why are you so interested in getting rid of Ultrix?  OK, it's old and
not as chiq as the fancy microkernel-based stuff, but it is still a
cosy little implementation of BSD :-)

