I'm confused: the 200 4/233 has a built-in network interface that uses
either thin or TP.  No AUI.  On these machines the driver will figure out
which of the two connections is live and use that one.  But you shouldn't
have anything connected to the other connector, e.g. don't terminate the
BNC connector if you're using the TP port.

The 200 4/233 is a pizza-box machine.  If you have a small tower box
then its Ethernet is provided on an option card.  In this case you'll
have three connections on the back of the Ethernet card.  If this is
the kind of machine you have then you'll need to move a jumper.  Open
the right-side of the box (the side away from the power switch.)  Find
the Ethernet card.  On it you'll see a big "gang jumper."  This selects
either the thin/TP connectors, or the thin/AUI.  It's silk-screened on
the board.


