I just got a very strange experience:

consider a REAL variable RBUF, a REAL array XREC(A), where A is an
INTEGER and set to some value, say 20 
      PARAMETER(A=20)

Now consider the intrinsic function LOG10: it takes one argument,
a REAL variable. 

My experience now is as follows:
I assign
      IF (XREC(A).GT.0.0) THEN 
        R=LOG10(XREC(A))
      ENDIF
and it works for several times on either
a DECstation running Ultrix 4.3a or DEC Alpha running OSF 1.2.
Then the system suddenly reports a floating point error, divide by zero.

I then compile and run the program on a PC 486 running Linux 1.1.73
and using f77 from the f2c package, and it works!

Now I assign the argument  to a buffer variable:
      IF (XREC(A).GT.0.0) THEN
        RBUF=XREC(A)
        R=LOG10(RBUF)
      ENDIF
and it works also on Ultrix and OSF systems!

My question now: Is this a bug or a feature?

Can DEC managers live in peace with the fact that a free 
FORTRAN compiler (f2c) works more intelligent than a 
commercial high-priced product :) ?

