Rick - I am involved in the integration of a VAXcluster-based system
that contains at least 48 RF36 drives in various shadow-set
arrangements.  It has taken us two years to assemble what we currently
have, and during that time we have not experienced a single RF36 failure.
We have also performed a reliability engineering analysis of the hardware
in the system.  The expected MTBF on an RF36 is far longer than your
described experiences would indicate, and is more in line with our
experience (50+ drive-years with no failures).  In fact, the RF36's
overall reliability and operation is far superior to the RA81s currently
being used in an older incarnation of our system, which are reaching
the end of their lifespan and dying almost predictably.

You've already mentioned that you have a power distribution unit - could
it be creating conditions under which your other (older) hardware can
operate fine, but that the RF36 finds damaging?  Could there be some
environmental factor (smoke, dust, ionizing radiation, pizza fumes,
tectonic activity, etc.) that could be unacceptable to your RF36s?  Again,
the engineering specs on the RF36 (except size, weight, power, and heat)
far exceed those of the other units you mentioned, so I really think it
must be something in your environment.  Also, I'm surprised that whoever
services your equipment hasn't asked similar questions.  That truly is
an appalling failure rate, and I'd hate to have to foot the bill.

Please post any developments in this area.  On behalf of our customer,
we are very interested in any reported reliability problems with the
RF36.  Thanks in advance...

