Dirk Grunwald (grunwald@widget.cs.colorado.edu) wrote:

: A number of companies sell inexpensive head-mounted displays, mainly
: intended for the PC market. I was interested in a $799 model that
: hooks up to an SVGA monitor output (the HMD is made by Virtual I/O).

: The AlphaStation series comes with a ZLXp-E1 graphics card, which is
: an 8-bit OpenGL-groking graphics adapter that (allegedly) uses SVGA
: signals to drive the monitor (or HMD).

: The Virtual I/O alternates images on the two LCD monitors on the HMD.
: Thus, to get 3-D effects, I need to do stero-rendering and interleave
: those images.

: This must be similar to the processe needed for 3-D stereo using e.g.,
: CrystalEyes. Does anyone have pointers for software to control this
: kind of process?

Video for CrystalEyes is a bit different.  It uses a field/eye-
interleave that, at first glance, seems strange.  In stereo mode,
the scene for one eye comes from the top-half of video memory
and the other eye from the bottom.  Each scanline then gets displayed
twice typically causing vertical stretching if one doesn't correct
the viewport mapping in the rendering pipeline and a 'modified'
vertical retrace is generated halfway through the vram scan.  I say
modified because only the special CrystalEyes monitors will recognize
it.  On a normal monitor with the graphics set to stereo mode, I
believe you will get two vertically-compressed screens, one on top
of the other (it's been awhile since I've done this).  The CrystalEyes
sender unit (an led picked up by the glasses) is driven by the
adapter's video clocking circuitry.  The reason for this type of
interleaving is that it retains memory contiguity in the scene in
both mono and stereo mode which is generally a large win for the
rendering hardware and that the LCD glasses can barely switch
in the vertical retrace interval.

The ZLXp-E1, which supports CrystalEyes, can be used at resolutions
down to 640x480 with separate H and V sync.  It sounds like the
i.glasses want interlacing on alternate scanlines which you are
going to have to implement in software or external hardware.  If
you wanted to use OpenGL for the stereoscopic images, you'll probably
have to use something like glXCreateGLXPixmap (does that work?) to
render off-screen then shuffle the resulting renderings into a
final image.  If you wanted to do Plain Old X (POX), you'll still
have to do pixmap shuffling.

: The combination of (admittedly low-res) HMD's with stereo sound (the
: VIO headset has headphones) and position-detection (VIO has that too)
: with the Alpha is fairly appealing for some VR-on-the-cheap work we'd
: like to do.

I'd like to hear how it goes and what you think of some of the
other inexpensive HMD's:  CyberEye, Forte's, and CyberMaxx.  I'm
considering the Forte unit but haven't gleaned much info from sci.vr.

