=Just copy the SYSUAF.DAT.  By backup to/from tape, or across a net.  Make sure
=you double check the protection on the resulting file and make sure its in
=sys$common, not sys$specific.  Check quotas on the SYSTEM and any other common
=accounts before and after, fix them, and you can use the differences as a guide
=to what you might need to change for all accounts.

Well, that works fine if you want to use all the users at once.  However, he
says he wants to move them in bunches.  In that case, moving SYSUAF.DAT
probably won't work:  You'll have folks who change their passwords after the
move has started but before it's finished.  In this case, you simply want to 
copy the records for the each of the users you want to move.  That's easy to
do.  One way to do it is via a FORTRAN program similar to the one that follows. 
Of course, you'll want to make a copy of the Alpha's SYSUAF.DAT before trying
anything like this.

	CHARACTER*2048 RECORD
	CHARACTER*32 USER
	INTEGER*4 RECLEN
	OPEN (UNIT=1,FILE='OLDUAF.DAT',ACCESS='KEYED',STATUS='OLD',
	1	FORM='FORMATTED')
	OPEN (UNIT=2,FILE='NEWUAF.DAT',ACCESS='KEYED',STATUS='OLD',SHARED,
	1	FORM='FORMATTED')
10	WRITE(6,20)
20	FORMAT(' Username: ',$)
	READ(5,30,END=50), USER
30	FORMAT(A)
	READ(1,40,KEY=USER) RECLEN, RECORD
40	FORMAT(Q,A)
	WRITE(2,30) RECORD(1:RECLEN)
	GOTO 10
50	END
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
