In article <ghooker-0902951506120001@wqbkgd.env.gov.bc.ca>, ghooker@epdiv1.env.gov.bc.ca (H. Gordon  Hooker) writes:
> Can an RRD-50 be hooked up to a Mac (or PC for that matter?)
> 
> I hooked it up to my Mac, was able to see it on the SCSI bus, but could
> not mount a CD.  (in fact, the access light doesn't even come on when a CD
> is put in).

  That's interesting. RRD50's use a proprietary LMS bus. RRD40's are avail-
able in either LMS or SCSI flavors, and all the newer DEC RRD4x drives are
SCSI only.

  DEC uses a 512 byte sector size, while most other vendors expect CD's to
have a 2048 byte size. That's probably your problem. See if your driver has
an option to work with 512 byte sectors.

  If you have an RRD42 and want to "de-DECify" it, open up the enclosure.
Inside you'll see several wires connecting the external ID switch to a con-
nector on the drive. On the connector that plugs into the drive, there's a
loop of wire (usually purple). Cut this wire and tape the ends to get an
instant Sony CDU-541.

