You just forgot to mention what TYPE of exa you are using.
In case you only tried the jukebox once (or so) at a different machine 
successfully, I would NOT think this does mean anything at all.

To my experience Exa's are just not very reliable, especcially the 8200 drives.

Also make sure you DO use cleaning cartridges frequently and do use cassettes 
marked for DIGITAL STORAGE, never any meant for videos (no matter how perfect they 
might be for that).

My experience showed only one problem with EXABYTE drives that is indeed correlated 
with the type of machine it is connected to: Copying large amounts of data (>1GB) 
from exa to exa on the same SCSI of a 4000 VAXstaion will crash the machine with a >50%
probability. DEC told me after analyzing a couple of crash-dumps that this is caused
by wrong timing on the SCSI bus.
No problem with AXPs in that respect so far, but no personal experience with 2100servers.

My (again very personal) advice: Use DLTs (designed for computing) or if they're too 
expensive DATs (designed at least for storage of *digital* audio data) rather than 
Video8 drives (designed for *analog* recording of grandma's 100th birthday)...

NO trouble whatsoever reported here in the institute with DLTs yet, even the
tUNIX (1) freaks here now dumped EXAs and DATs and recommend DLTs despite they come 
from DEC and might smell VMS-ish.....

Uwe Leinberger, GSI (Inst. for Heavy Ion Physics), Darmsdat, Germany


