In article <1995Feb2.011036.5972@microunity.com>, wampler@microunity.com (Kurt Wampler) writes:
> 31457@chopin.udel.edu (James D Rosemary) writes:
> 
>>I am writing a VT100 emulator (for my own personal use - I am the sole
>>owner of a machine that it will run on). Does anyone know where I can
>>find info on escape sequences, etc?  I've found a few by scripting vi,
>>but I need more info. 
> 
>   Here's a list of VT100 escape sequences.
> 
>   - Kurt
> ================================================================================
> Taken from VT100 Programming Reference Card (DIGITAL)
> 
> ANSI Compatible Mode
> 
> 	Cursor Movement Commands
>		[...]
> 		Direct cursor addressing	ESC [ Pl; Pc H	or
> 						ESC [ Pl; Pc f
> 		* Pn = decimal parameter in string of ASCII digits.(default 1)
> 		* Pl = line number (default 0); Pc = column number (default 0)

Note:	if Pl or Pc is 0, the corresponding cursor coordinate is unchanged.

> 	Scrolling Region
> 		ESC [ Pt ; Pb r
> 		Pt is the number of the top line of the scrolling region;
> 		Pb is the number of the bottom line of the scrolling region 
> 		and must be greater than  Pt.

Note:	If Pt is 0 or omitted, it defaults to 1.  If Pb is 0 or omitted,
	it defaults to the end of the screen (24 on a real VT100).

You may also want to implement some of the additional parameters for the
ESC [ pn m sequence - for example, to clear individual attributes and/or
to set colors.  Quite a few programs like to be able to use them:

	Clear bold		ESC [ 22 m
	Clear underline		ESC [ 24 m
	Clear Blink		ESC [ 25 m
	Clear Reverse		ESC [ 27 m
	Black foreground	ESC [ 30 m
	Red foreground		ESC [ 31 m
	Green foreground	ESC [ 32 m
	Yellow foreground	ESC [ 33 m
	Blue foreground		ESC [ 34 m
	Magenta foreground	ESC [ 35 m
	Cyan foreground		ESC [ 36 m
	White foreground	ESC [ 37 m
	Black background	ESC [ 40 m
	Red background		ESC [ 41 m
	Green background	ESC [ 42 m
	Yellow background	ESC [ 43 m
	Blue background		ESC [ 44 m
	Magenta background	ESC [ 45 m
	Cyan background		ESC [ 46 m
	White background	ESC [ 47 m

As with other values for Pn, these can be combined together with ;'s.
Note that Clear Bold is 22, not 21 as you might expect.  On some
non-DEC hardware, there is a Dim attribute which is attribute 2, and
Pn=22 clears both the Bold and Dim attributes.

Good luck,

Bruce C. Wright


