In article <lloyd-dr-0302950907550001@149.252.239.58>,
  lloyd-dr@nlvvax.llnl.egg.nv.doe.gov (David Lloyd) writes:
> All right, I've apparently gone brain-dead, but I cannot for the life of me
>get the following C example to compile with DEC C 4.0 under OpenVMS/VAX V6.1.
> I get the warning message (between the dashed lines) that says (to me anyway)
>"a" is not compatible with "a". Can anyone tell me where my programming and/or
>linguistic skills left the room (with my sanity not far behind)?
>
>/* Program that does no useful work */
>
>#include <stdlib.h>
>
>int compare (int *a, int *b)
>{
>  if (a < b) return(-1);
>  if (a == b) return(0);
>  if (a > b) return(1);
>}
>
>void main(int argc, char** argv)
>{
>
>  int intarray[10] = {4,3,6,2,1,7,9,8,3,1};
>
>  qsort(intarray, 10, sizeof(int), compare);
>
>  return;
>}


The stdlib header file defines the last argument to qsort as a pointer
to a funtion that take arguments (const void *, const void *).  The
compiler complains because you are passing a function with arguments
(int *, int *).

One workaround is to add the declaration "int compare();" to your main()
function, the old fashioned function declaration disables the type checking
on the arguments.

BTW, the compare() routine in your sample is going to compare the addresses
of the data items, not the values.


