Hello everyone. 

Recently in my research group we got a new Alphastation 200 4/233.
I'm telling those on the net of my experiences installing and
testing it.

Configuration:
PCI bus,
64M ram, 
10baseT ethernet interface, 
Alpha 21064A 233 MHz processor
1G SCSI disk, FAST interface
17inch SVGA monitor, 8-plane graphics (ZLXP-E1)


Installation:

    I had very little trouble putting the machine together and getting it
up and running.  The instructions, while sparse, were sufficient.
The only problem I had was with the ethernet:  my department had not
turned on our ethernet connection, so I was wondering what installation
mistake I had committed installing the dec.  Once the ethernet port was
turned on, the machine performed flawlessly.

Look and feel:

  The monitor is a very nice one, 17 inches SVGA, 8-plane graphics.  The
X-performance is *very* snappy.  The case was *slightly* misaligned when
the machine arrived, but my opinion is that it'd take someone very picky
to regard this as a serious flaw.  (Especially since it took 10 seconds to
fix.)

  The keyboard is a PC keyboard, not DEC's traditional keyboard.  However,
it has a nicer feel (in my humble opinion) than other DEC keyboards, and
actually has an escape key.  The Caps lock key is ridiculously placed for
a UNIX machine and the control key remote, however if you use xkeycaps,
this isn't a problem....

  The internal SCSI drive is a bit loud.

  I really like the machine.

Benchmarking:  

Unscientific, I was on multiuser machines operating on ethernet.
However, I was the only user on the machine....


Compile:    complete g++ build on a 15000 line code

AlphaStation:	12minutes, 10 seconds over NFS
	         6minutes, 50 seconds on local disk

Sparc 2:        20minutes, 6 seconds over NFS
		*didn't have a chance to compile locally on the sparc2 yet*

This result is actually pretty disappointing.  The probable reason why
is because of disk-intensive links--a SCSI drive is only so fast.
The big lesson is that as much compilation should be done locally
as possible.....


Numerical simulation code:

Speedup of Alphastation		from

code:  xoopic compiled with g++ -O2 (our plasma simulation code)

6x				Sparc 2

  From SPECfp, I expected a 8x increase in performance.  

code:  xpdp2, gcc -O2 or cc -O2  (not sure which, it was someone else's program)

2x				DEC 3000/300

  From SPECfp, I expected 2x increase.



Hope you find this interesting and useful,

Peter Mardahl





