Hi;

We have a small site. The site consists of a 6420, 7610 and 4200 all (running 
vms5.5-2) and approx 40 disks. A few of these are old RA and new RZ drives. 
28 of them are RF series drives. 12 RF35, 12 RF36 and varying RF31 models. Our 
oldest RF drives are 4 years old (rf30x). The oldest RF35/36 drives are approx 
2 years old and the newest are approx 6mo to 1  year old (varies).  All 
(except 1 of the RF35 and RF36 drives) are mounted in a SF400 cab and are 
relatively split on a 1/3 2/3 basis between the 7610 and 6420 respectively. 
They are not dual hosted but merely share the same box. All other rf drives 
are internally mounted in the 4200 or in a BA350 cab.

During this time we have lost 1 RF35 drive due to failure and none of the 
RF30x drives. None of the rf30x (4 years) have ever even shown an error. 

Over this past year we have had an appalling failure rate on the RF36 drives. 
Within the past 6 months or so we have lost around 10-12 drives (luckily we've 
avoided losing data and I admit I'm counting one DOA replacement). Not all of 
the drives have failed mind you. In some cases the replacement drives have 
died. In the past 10 days we have lost 3 (one replacement died within 7 days - 
on the button). All drives are at current firmware rev. levels according to 
DEC field service. All drives pass onboard firmware diagnostics and 
'analyze/disk'  and have worked 'fine' until the point of failure - by that I 
mean we have had - in all cases - only 5 or ten minutes warning between the 
time the first errors started to appear and it was dead. There wasn't any 
history of ongoing/occasional  errors being logged.

We have checked the power source without problem and all drives (all the 
equipment actually) share a common power source (from a distribution system).

Sorry for the lengthy background and I apologize for not being able to tell 
you what the typical op rate is on the drives but I don't have the figures at 
hand.  They are high but our old (soon to be) discarded  RA81 and RA82 drives 
were quite able to withstand the load (and we thought they had reliability 
problems losing one or two a year in total). While I'd appreciate any isnite 
as to the cause that people might have I am more curious as to whether any one 
else has had either similar problems or the opposite circumstance of good/high 
reliability.

