>I am trying to reconfigure an old Fujitsu drive with a controller that 
>wants to make it look like a dec RM80.  Can anybody tell me the specs on 
>the RM80 drive: # of cylinders, # of sects/per cyclinder, # of heads.

32 Sectors (0-31) 16-Bit Word Format, 30 Sectors (0-29) 18-Bit Word Format
Sector 31 in 16-Bit Word Format reserved as a replacement for bad sectors.
Max cylinders is 561, physical cylinders 560 and 561 are service 
cylinders for diagnostic use. The skip-sector file resides at cylinder 
560, track 0. Data files should not be stored on these cylinders. Other 
parts of the manual have comments like "In no instance should the user 
attempt to write into a reserved sector or into any of the service (FE) 
<<Field Engineering?>> cylinders (559 and 560)."

16-Bit word format               124M Byte
Number of heads                  14 data, 1 servo
Number of tracks                 561 per head (0-560)
Number of sectors
   16-bit word format            30 sectors (Page 1-12)
                                 31 sectors (Page 1-4)
                                 32 sectors (Page 1-6)

512 Bytes of data per cylinder, 16-Bit Words format.

All the above has been taken from the RM80 Disk Drive User's Guide 
EK-ORM80-UG-002, don't be surprised if numbers appear to be a little bit 
rubbery when it comes to the sector format. Sorry about the confusion.

Regards,
Garry Page




