Yep.  Just use Ultrix.

We use older Ultrix workstations as X window terminals all the time.  Here
is the entry in our /etc/ttys file:

:0 "/etc/Xws_with_100dpi" none on secure

And here is the Xws_with_100dpi file (I have others for differing situations):

#!/usr/bin/ksh
#
# Startup the X server.  In the case of a DECstation 5000/133 this is Xws.
#
# This file sets the font path to the DECwindow 100dpi directory plus
# the directory containing miscellaneous fonts and the MIT supplied fonts.
#
exec /usr/bin/Xws bc  \
-fp /usr/lib/X11/fonts/decwin/100dpi,\
/usr/lib/X11/fonts/misc,\
/usr/lib/X11/fonts/MIT \
-class 1280x1024 -query pt9500 \
-terminate\
   >> /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xws-errors 2>> /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xws-errors

Note the -class and the -query tell the server to use an xdm display class and
query another host for login via xdmcp. You may need to change the font path
(fp) switches accordingly.

This is under Ultrix 4.4. Earlier versions of Xws (or whatever your local
X server is called) used different switches for its xdm configuration.

