In article <blsi.2.00084401@blsi.slip.iglou.com>, blsi@blsi.slip.iglou.com
(Bob Nevins) writes:
:What I need to know seems simple, however, I have not been able to receive a 
:firm answer:  
:
:        Can a DEC Alpha PC-LAN server (Alpha 2000-300 chip) be a server 
:        for Windows NT?

    Yes.

    When sold with NT or NTAS, the DEC 2000 model 300 system was often
    confusingly refered to as the DECpc AXP 150 -- it's the same box.

    The DEC 2000 model 300 can run Microsoft Windows NT (and NTAS), and
    it can also run Pathworks on the OpenVMS Alpha or on OSF/1 Alpha
    operating systems.  Any of these options will allow the DEC 2000
    model 300 to serve Windows NT clients.

    When/if switching operating systems, check the support for the SVGA
    graphics controllers, as only a subset of the available graphics
    controllers are supported across all operating systems.  Check the
    other hardware as well, but the SVGA is often the biggest sticking
    point.  (The Qvision controller is probably the best current choice
    for OS portability.)  You will also need the appropriate version of
    the PALcode and ECU for the OS in use -- OpenVMS and OSF/1 share one
    set of PALcode, while NT/NTAS has its own PALcode version.

